insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 7.1 investigations, evidence, chain of custody (ci-q-1500..1511)
('ci-q-1500','cissp',7,'7.1','mcq',1,$q$A signed contract recovered from a suspect's filing cabinet is offered in court to prove the terms of an agreement. What category of evidence is this document?$q$,$q$["Documentary evidence","Testimonial evidence","Demonstrative evidence","Corroborative-only evidence"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Documentary evidence is any written or recorded material - contracts, business records, printed logs, emails - offered to prove the facts it contains. Testimonial evidence is spoken statements under oath; demonstrative evidence illustrates or recreates a fact (a chart or diagram) rather than being the fact itself.$q$),
('ci-q-1501','cissp',7,'7.1','mcq',2,$q$A prosecutor wants to introduce the contents of a contract into evidence. Under the best evidence rule, what is generally required?$q$,$q$["The original contract itself, or an acceptable justification for why only a duplicate is available","Only a verbal summary of the contract from a witness who once read it","A notarized photograph of the document is always required instead of the original","The rule only applies to digital records, never to paper documents"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The best evidence rule requires the original document (or a legally recognized duplicate) rather than testimony describing its contents, because an original is the most reliable proof of what it actually says. Courts do accept duplicates when the original is lost, destroyed without bad faith, or otherwise unobtainable, but a bare verbal summary is not a substitute when the original could reasonably be produced, and the rule is not limited to paper records.$q$),
('ci-q-1502','cissp',7,'7.1','mcq',2,$q$A company's automatically generated authentication log, created by the system itself at the time of each login attempt as part of routine operations, is offered in court to show when an account was accessed. Why does this log typically avoid exclusion under the hearsay rule?$q$,$q$["It qualifies for the business records exception, since it was created and kept in the ordinary course of regularly conducted business activity","Computer-generated logs are never considered hearsay under any circumstance because they are not human statements","The log is admissible only if the system administrator personally witnessed every login","Hearsay rules apply only to spoken testimony and never to any written record"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Hearsay is generally an out-of-court statement offered to prove the truth of what it asserts, but records made and kept in the regular course of business - such as system-generated authentication logs - fall under the business records exception when a proper foundation (regular practice, contemporaneous creation) is laid. Whether a record is "computer-generated" doesn't automatically exempt it from hearsay analysis; it still has to satisfy a recognized exception like this one.$q$),
('ci-q-1503','cissp',7,'7.1','mcq',1,$q$For evidence to be admissible in a legal proceeding, it generally must satisfy which three criteria?$q$,$q$["Relevant to the matter at hand, reliable/competent, and legally obtained","Expensive to collect, digital in format, and stored on encrypted media","Collected only by a sworn law-enforcement officer, regardless of context","Older than one year and previously published publicly"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Courts generally assess admissibility against relevance (does it actually help prove or disprove a fact at issue), reliability/competence (is it accurate and was it properly authenticated and handled), and legality of how it was obtained (no unlawful search, coercion, or other rights violation). Cost, format, and age are not admissibility criteria on their own.$q$),
('ci-q-1504','cissp',7,'7.1','mcq',2,$q$An intruder breaks into a server room, leaving a fingerprint on a console and unknowingly carrying away carpet fibers on their shoes, while also picking up trace residue from the room on their clothing. What forensic principle explains why this kind of mutual trace transfer is expected at any physical crime scene?$q$,$q$["Locard's exchange principle","The best evidence rule","The chain of custody requirement","The hearsay exception for present-sense impressions"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Locard's exchange principle holds that every contact between two objects or people leaves a mutual trace - the intruder takes something away (fibers, residue) and leaves something behind (a fingerprint) - which is why physical crime scenes are systematically searched for trace evidence in both directions. It is a forensic science principle, not a rule about document authenticity or evidence-handling documentation.$q$),
('ci-q-1505','cissp',7,'7.1','mcq',3,$q$A security team sets up a realistic-looking but fake file share, publicly reachable, that any authenticated user could stumble across and access out of curiosity - and logs whoever connects to it, purely to observe unauthorized-access attempts already likely to occur. A separate case involves an undercover investigator repeatedly messaging a suspect, pressuring and persuading them for weeks to commit a crime they had shown no prior inclination toward. How should these two scenarios be characterized?$q$,$q$["The file share is lawful enticement, since it merely offers an opportunity an inclined actor would take on their own; the repeated pressuring is entrapment, since it induces someone to commit a crime they were not already predisposed to commit","Both scenarios are entrapment and therefore legally impermissible","Both scenarios are lawful enticement and therefore always usable as evidence","Entrapment only applies to physical crime scenes, never to digital investigations"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Enticement offers an opportunity to someone already inclined to break the law - a decoy resource that does nothing to push a curious or malicious actor toward acting - and evidence gathered from it is generally usable. Entrapment happens when investigators induce or persuade someone into committing a crime they weren't already predisposed to commit, which can bar prosecution on that basis. The distinction turns on predisposition and inducement, and it applies equally to digital honeypot-style scenarios and traditional undercover work.$q$),
('ci-q-1506','cissp',7,'7.1','mcq',2,$q$A workstation is suspected of running memory-resident malware that would vanish if the machine were powered off, but investigators also need a forensically sound disk image for later analysis. What approach best balances these needs?$q$,$q$["Capture volatile memory and relevant live system state first (live forensics), then power down and create a write-blocked, hashed disk image (dead-box/static forensics) for preservation and analysis","Immediately power off the machine to preserve the disk exactly as it is, since memory contents are never useful as evidence","Only ever perform live forensics, since powering off a system always destroys legally admissible evidence","Only ever perform dead-box forensics, since live analysis always contaminates the disk beyond use"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Live (volatile) forensics captures evidence that would otherwise be lost the moment power is removed, such as memory-resident malware, open network connections, and running processes, while dead-box (static) forensics on a write-blocked, hashed image of the powered-down disk yields the most defensible, unalterable long-term evidentiary record. Neither approach alone captures everything; combining them, live acquisition first, then a static image, is the balanced practice.$q$),
('ci-q-1507','cissp',7,'7.1','mcq',1,$q$What must a search warrant specify to satisfy the particularity requirement before investigators may lawfully search a suspect's premises or seize their devices?$q$,$q$["The specific place to be searched and the specific items or evidence being sought","Only the name of the requesting investigator, with no other detail required","The total dollar amount of expected damages in the case","Nothing beyond a general statement that a crime may have occurred somewhere"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Particularity requires a warrant to describe with reasonable specificity both the place to be searched and the items or evidence sought, preventing open-ended, exploratory searches beyond what a judge actually authorized. A warrant this vague that it names neither a place nor the evidence sought would not meet that standard.$q$),
('ci-q-1508','cissp',7,'7.1','mcq',3,$q$An employer's IT staff, acting entirely on their own initiative and using authority granted by the company's own acceptable use policy, search an employee's company-issued laptop for evidence of policy violations, with no law-enforcement involvement or direction. The employee later argues the search violated their constitutional rights. Why does this argument typically fail?$q$,$q$["Constitutional search-and-seizure protections restrict government action; a purely private-sector search conducted without government direction or participation generally does not trigger those protections","Private companies are legally required to obtain a warrant before searching any company-owned device","Constitutional protections apply identically to private employers and government investigators in every case","The employee's argument succeeds automatically whenever a personal device is involved"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Constitutional search-and-seizure protections constrain government actors, not private parties acting independently; a private employer searching its own equipment under its own policy, without acting as an agent of or in coordination with law enforcement, generally falls outside that protection (though the search may still be governed by employment law, contract, or the AUP itself). That analysis would change if law enforcement directed or effectively took over the search.$q$),
('ci-q-1509','cissp',7,'7.1','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO items below that are examples of REAL (physical/tangible) evidence, as distinct from documentary or testimonial evidence.$q$,$q$["A hard drive physically seized from a suspect's desk","A firearm recovered from a crime scene","A witness's spoken account of what they observed","A written policy document describing company procedures"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Real evidence is a tangible physical object connected to the case - a seized hard drive or a recovered firearm both qualify. A witness's spoken account is testimonial evidence, and a written policy document is documentary evidence; both are distinct evidentiary categories from real evidence even though all four might appear in the same case.$q$),
('ci-q-1510','cissp',7,'7.1','ordering',2,$q$Order the general sequence of sound evidence handling at a digital crime scene, from the first action to the last.$q$,$q$["Secure and isolate the scene to prevent further contamination or tampering","Photograph and document the evidence and its surroundings in place before touching anything","Collect the evidence using proper forensic procedure, such as write-blocked imaging and cryptographic hashing","Label, tag, and log the evidence into a documented chain of custody","Transport and store the evidence in tamper-evident, access-controlled packaging","Perform analysis only against a verified working copy, never the preserved original"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4,5]$q$::jsonb,$q$Sound evidence handling begins with securing the scene so nothing further changes, documenting evidence in its original position before any collection begins, collecting it through forensically defensible methods that preserve integrity, formally logging it into custody, storing it securely, and finally analyzing only a verified copy so the original stays untouched for potential re-examination or court presentation - reordering these steps (e.g., collecting before documenting) undermines the evidentiary record.$q$),
('ci-q-1511','cissp',7,'7.1','matching',2,$q$Match each evidence-handling or legal concept on the left to its correct description on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Best evidence rule","Business records exception","Locard's exchange principle","Enticement"],"right":["Holds that any contact between two people or objects leaves a mutual trace - evidence carried away and evidence left behind","Offers an opportunity to someone already inclined to break the law, without inducing or pressuring them into it","Requires production of an original document, or a justified substitute, rather than testimony merely describing its contents","Lets a record made and kept in the ordinary course of regular business activity avoid exclusion under the hearsay rule"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$The best evidence rule prefers originals over descriptions of a document; the business records exception carves routine business records out of the hearsay bar; Locard's exchange principle is the forensic-science observation that contact always leaves mutual trace evidence; and enticement is the lawful counterpart to entrapment, offering rather than inducing an opportunity to offend.$q$),
-- 7.2 logging and monitoring (ci-q-1512..1523)
('ci-q-1512','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',1,$q$What is the core function of a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) platform?$q$,$q$["Aggregating and normalizing log data from many sources, correlating events across them, and generating alerts on suspicious patterns","Physically replacing failed network hardware","Encrypting data at rest across all managed endpoints","Approving change requests before production deployment"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A SIEM centralizes log collection from across the environment, normalizes disparate formats into a common structure, correlates related events that would look harmless in isolation, and raises alerts when correlated patterns match known or suspicious behavior - turning scattered raw logs into actionable security visibility. It performs none of the hardware, encryption, or change-approval functions listed as distractors.$q$),
('ci-q-1513','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',2,$q$An analyst wants to determine which external domain a compromised workstation attempted to contact just before it began exfiltrating data. Which log source is most directly useful for this?$q$,$q$["DNS resolution logs, showing which domain names the workstation queried","Physical badge access logs for the building","Payroll system audit logs","Print server job logs"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$DNS logs record which domain names a host resolved, directly revealing command-and-control or exfiltration destinations a workstation attempted to reach - exactly the artifact needed here. Badge access, payroll, and print logs are legitimate log sources for other purposes, but none show outbound domain lookups.$q$),
('ci-q-1514','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',1,$q$Why is accurate time synchronization (e.g., via NTP) across servers, network devices, and endpoints essential to effective log monitoring?$q$,$q$["Correlating events from multiple systems into a single accurate incident timeline depends on their timestamps actually agreeing with each other","Time synchronization primarily improves disk read/write speed on logging servers","Log files cannot be stored at all unless every system shares an identical clock","NTP encrypts log traffic in transit between systems"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$If a firewall, a server, and an IDS each timestamp events using clocks that have drifted apart, reconstructing the true order of a multi-system incident becomes unreliable or impossible - an event that a drifted clock shows as happening "before" another may have actually happened after. NTP keeps clocks aligned so cross-system correlation and incident timelines are trustworthy; it has nothing to do with disk performance or log encryption.$q$),
('ci-q-1515','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',2,$q$A monitoring policy states that up to 3 failed login attempts from a single account within 10 minutes are logged silently, but the 4th attempt in that window triggers an alert to the security team. What monitoring concept does this threshold represent?$q$,$q$["A clipping level, which suppresses routine/expected activity below a defined threshold while still alerting once that threshold is crossed","A honeypot, designed to lure and study attackers","A change window, restricting when production changes may occur","A business records exception under the hearsay rule"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A clipping level sets an acceptable threshold for routine, expected activity (a handful of mistyped passwords isn't inherently suspicious) so it doesn't generate alert noise, while still triggering an alert once the threshold is exceeded and the activity looks more like an actual attack rather than ordinary human error. It's unrelated to honeypots, change windows, or evidentiary hearsay rules.$q$),
('ci-q-1516','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',2,$q$How does Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response (SOAR) typically extend what a SIEM alone provides?$q$,$q$["SOAR consumes SIEM alerts and automatically executes predefined response playbooks - such as isolating a host or disabling an account - reducing manual analyst response time","SOAR replaces the need for any log collection or correlation entirely","SOAR is simply another name for the same SIEM correlation functionality","SOAR's only function is generating the visual dashboards a SIEM displays"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SIEM's job is detection - aggregating, correlating, and alerting on log data. SOAR builds on top of that by orchestrating automated, predefined response actions (isolating an endpoint, disabling a compromised account, opening a ticket) once an alert fires, shrinking the time between detection and containment. It is a complementary layer, not a substitute for log collection/correlation or merely a dashboarding tool.$q$),
('ci-q-1517','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',3,$q$A monitoring tool builds a behavioral baseline for each user - typical login times, typical source locations, typical data volumes accessed - and flags an account when its activity deviates sharply from that individual baseline, such as a login from a country the user has never connected from immediately after a login from their home city. What type of tool is this?$q$,$q$["User and Entity Behavior Analytics (UEBA)","A signature-based antivirus engine","A packet-filtering firewall","A configuration management database"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$UEBA builds statistical behavioral profiles per user or entity and flags meaningful deviations from that individual baseline - such as the classic "impossible travel" pattern - catching account compromise or insider misuse that wouldn't match any known malware signature or static rule. Signature antivirus, packet filtering, and a CMDB all serve different, unrelated purposes.$q$),
('ci-q-1518','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',2,$q$An employee attempts to copy a spreadsheet containing sensitive customer records onto a personal USB drive, and an agent running on the laptop itself blocks the copy operation before any file is written to the drive. Which DLP enforcement point and data state does this scenario represent?$q$,$q$["Endpoint DLP, protecting data-in-use at the point of local file operations such as copy-to-removable-media","Network DLP, protecting data-in-motion as it crosses the network perimeter","Cloud DLP enforced by a CASB, protecting data stored in a SaaS application","Database-level DLP, protecting data-at-rest inside a database engine"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Endpoint DLP agents run directly on the host and can intercept local actions - copying to removable media, printing, clipboard operations - that never touch the network at all, which is exactly the data-in-use scenario here. Network DLP inspects traffic crossing the wire, cloud/CASB DLP governs SaaS application data, and database DLP protects stored records - none of which would see a purely local USB copy attempt.$q$),
('ci-q-1519','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',3,$q$A DLP policy needs to detect outbound transmissions containing a specific, known confidential engineering document, even if only an excerpted portion of it is pasted into an email, while a separate rule needs to catch any outbound message containing a pattern that structurally resembles a Social Security number. Which two DLP detection techniques respectively fit these two goals?$q$,$q$["Document fingerprinting for the known document, and pattern/regex matching for the SSN-shaped data","Regex matching for both scenarios, since fingerprinting cannot detect partial content","Document fingerprinting for both scenarios, since regex cannot detect structured numeric patterns","Machine learning classification is required for both, since neither simpler technique can address either goal"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Document fingerprinting hashes a known sensitive document (or sections of it) so DLP can recognize that exact content, or a meaningful excerpt of it, appearing elsewhere - well suited to protecting a specific known file. Pattern/regex matching looks for content that structurally matches a known format, such as the digit pattern of an SSN, regardless of which specific document it came from - well suited to detecting a category of sensitive data rather than one particular file. Each technique fits a different kind of detection goal.$q$),
('ci-q-1520','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',2,$q$A DLP system detects an email containing regulated health data addressed to an external recipient and automatically applies encryption to the message before allowing it to send, rather than blocking it outright. What does this illustrate about DLP policy actions?$q$,$q$["DLP responses exist on a spectrum from monitor/alert-only through automatic remediation (such as forced encryption) to an outright block, chosen based on policy and business need rather than being limited to a single all-or-nothing response","DLP tools can only ever block a transmission outright, with no other possible response","Encryption is not a valid DLP response and indicates a system malfunction","DLP policy actions are identical across every organization and cannot be configured"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$DLP policies commonly support a graduated range of responses - silently logging/alerting on low-risk matches, automatically remediating (such as forcing encryption or stripping an attachment) for legitimate business communications that still need extra protection, and outright blocking for clear policy violations - letting an organization tune the response to the actual risk and business workflow rather than forcing every match into a single block-or-allow outcome.$q$),
('ci-q-1521','cissp',7,'7.2','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements below that correctly describe why SIEM correlation adds value beyond reviewing individual log sources separately.$q$,$q$["A failed VPN login from an unusual country followed minutes later by a successful login and a large database export, each unremarkable alone across different log sources, becomes a clear attack pattern once correlated together","Correlation can surface a multi-stage attack pattern that no single log source shows in isolation","Correlation eliminates the need to retain any raw log data once an alert has fired","Correlation guarantees that every alert generated represents a genuine incident with no false positives"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$The core value of correlation is connecting individually unremarkable events across different sources and time into a recognizable attack pattern that no single log would reveal alone - the VPN-login-then-export example is a textbook case. Correlation does not remove the need to retain raw logs (they remain essential for investigation and compliance), and no correlation engine eliminates false positives entirely; tuning reduces but never guarantees away noise.$q$),
('ci-q-1522','cissp',7,'7.2','ordering',2,$q$Order the typical SIEM log-processing lifecycle, from the first stage to the last.$q$,$q$["Collect and ingest raw log data from configured sources","Normalize the data into a consistent common format","Correlate related events across sources against defined rules or behavioral baselines","Generate an alert when a correlation match or threshold is met","Retain the log data per the organization's retention policy for future investigation or audit"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$A SIEM first ingests raw logs from its configured sources, normalizes their varied native formats into a consistent structure so they can be compared, correlates related events against rules or behavioral baselines, fires an alert once a match or threshold condition is met, and finally retains the underlying data per policy so it remains available for later investigation, forensics, or compliance review - skipping normalization, for instance, would make cross-source correlation unreliable.$q$),
('ci-q-1523','cissp',7,'7.2','matching',3,$q$Match each log source on the left to what it most directly reveals to an investigator.$q$,$q${"left":["DNS resolution logs","Web proxy logs","Authentication logs","Firewall logs"],"right":["Which internal user accounts experienced failed or successful login attempts, and when","Which domain names a host attempted to resolve, often revealing command-and-control destinations","Which specific external web addresses and destinations a user's browser actually requested","Which network connections were permitted or blocked at the network perimeter, by source, destination, and port"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,2,0,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each log source has a distinct evidentiary strength: DNS logs reveal domain-level lookups, often the first sign of malware phoning home; proxy logs reveal the actual web destinations and content requests a browser made; authentication logs reveal login activity and account-level anomalies; and firewall logs reveal what traffic crossed the network boundary and whether it was allowed or denied - an investigator typically needs several of these together to reconstruct a full incident timeline.$q$),
-- 7.3 configuration management (ci-q-1524..1533)
('ci-q-1524','cissp',7,'7.3','mcq',1,$q$What is a baseline configuration in the context of configuration management?$q$,$q$["A documented, approved reference state defining a system's settings, installed software, and security controls, against which its actual state can later be compared","A one-time inventory count of hardware assets performed during initial deployment only","The default factory settings a vendor ships with a device, left unmodified","A list of every historical change ever made to a system, in chronological order"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A baseline configuration is the formally approved, documented "known-good" state - covering settings, installed software, patch level, and security controls - that a system is expected to match. It exists specifically so the current, live state of a system can be compared against it to detect unauthorized deviation; it is not simply a one-time inventory snapshot, unmodified factory defaults (which are rarely secure as shipped), or a change log.$q$),
('ci-q-1525','cissp',7,'7.3','mcq',2,$q$Over several months, individual administrators apply ad hoc fixes directly to a production server outside the change management process - installing a utility here, adjusting a firewall rule there - without updating the documented baseline. What has happened to this server?$q$,$q$["Configuration drift, where the system's actual live state has diverged from its documented, approved baseline","Data remanence, where residual data persists after supposed deletion","Privilege escalation, where an attacker gained higher permissions than intended","Business continuity failure, where a critical function became unavailable"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Configuration drift is exactly this accumulation of undocumented, unauthorized, or ad hoc changes that cause a system's real-world state to gradually diverge from its approved baseline - often through good-faith quick fixes that were never fed back into change management or the baseline documentation, leaving the environment progressively less predictable and harder to secure or audit.$q$),
('ci-q-1526','cissp',7,'7.3','mcq',1,$q$What do CIS Benchmarks provide to organizations building hardened system configurations?$q$,$q$["Consensus-based, vendor-neutral best-practice configuration guidance for securely configuring a wide range of operating systems, applications, and network devices","A legally binding government mandate that all organizations must follow exactly","A proprietary vulnerability scanner sold exclusively by one vendor","A certification that a specific piece of hardware is physically tamper-resistant"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$CIS Benchmarks, published by the Center for Internet Security, are consensus-developed, detailed hardening guides covering specific settings for operating systems, applications, cloud platforms, and network devices - a widely referenced starting point for building a secure baseline configuration. They are voluntary best-practice guidance rather than a binding legal mandate, and they are not a scanning tool or a hardware certification themselves, though scanning tools are often used to check compliance against them.$q$),
('ci-q-1527','cissp',7,'7.3','mcq',2,$q$As part of building a hardened server baseline, an administrator disables unused network services, removes default vendor accounts, and closes unnecessary open ports before the system goes into production. What security principle does this work most directly implement?$q$,$q$["Attack surface reduction, by minimizing the number of exposed services and accounts an attacker could target","Separation of duties, by dividing critical tasks among multiple people","Non-repudiation, by ensuring actions can be tied to a specific individual","Data minimization, by reducing the volume of personal data collected"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Every running service, open port, and enabled default account is a potential entry point; disabling what isn't actually needed shrinks the attack surface an adversary can probe or exploit, which is the direct goal of this hardening work. Separation of duties, non-repudiation, and data minimization are all legitimate but unrelated security principles that this specific set of actions does not address.$q$),
('ci-q-1528','cissp',7,'7.3','mcq',2,$q$An organization builds a single fully hardened, pre-configured server image - all required software installed, unnecessary services disabled, security settings applied per the approved baseline - and deploys every new production server from a copy of that same image. What benefit does this "golden image" approach provide over manually configuring each new server from scratch?$q$,$q$["It ensures every new system starts from an identical, already-hardened, known-good state, reducing the inconsistency and configuration drift that manual, one-off setup tends to introduce","It eliminates the need for any future patching of deployed systems","It guarantees the image never needs to be updated once it has been created","It removes the need for a documented baseline configuration entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A golden image bakes the approved baseline directly into the deployment artifact, so every new instance starts identically hardened rather than depending on a human correctly repeating dozens of manual steps every time - directly reducing both initial misconfiguration and the inconsistency that invites later drift. It does not eliminate the ongoing need to patch deployed systems or periodically refresh the image itself, and the image is itself an expression of the documented baseline, not a replacement for having one.$q$),
('ci-q-1529','cissp',7,'7.3','mcq',3,$q$An organization uses a declarative configuration management tool (such as Ansible, Puppet, or Chef) that continuously compares each managed server's live state to its defined baseline and automatically reverts any unauthorized change back to the approved state. What operational problem does this design most directly address?$q$,$q$["Configuration drift, by automatically detecting and remediating deviation from the baseline rather than relying on periodic manual audits to catch it","Data-in-transit interception, by encrypting traffic between servers","Insider threat recruitment, by monitoring employee communications","Vendor lock-in, by standardizing on a single cloud provider"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Declarative configuration tools define the desired end state and continuously (or on a scheduled interval) reconcile the actual system back to it, catching and correcting drift automatically and far faster than a periodic manual configuration audit would - directly attacking the root problem of systems silently diverging from their approved baseline over time. It has no direct bearing on traffic encryption, insider-threat monitoring, or cloud vendor selection.$q$),
('ci-q-1530','cissp',7,'7.3','mcq',2,$q$What is the primary purpose of the Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP)?$q$,$q$["To provide a standardized language and set of specifications enabling automated tools to check a system's configuration and vulnerability state against a defined security baseline","To physically encrypt data at rest on managed endpoints","To replace the need for a documented change management process","To serve as a backup rotation scheme for critical servers"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SCAP defines a common, machine-readable way to express security checks, configuration baselines, and vulnerability data so automated scanning tools from different vendors can consistently evaluate whether a system's actual configuration matches a defined standard - standardizing compliance and vulnerability checking rather than performing encryption, replacing change management, or handling backups.$q$),
('ci-q-1531','cissp',7,'7.3','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO actions below that are examples of system hardening intended to reduce a server's attack surface before it enters production.$q$,$q$["Removing or disabling default vendor accounts and their default passwords","Uninstalling or disabling network services the server does not actually need to provide","Increasing the number of administrative accounts with full privileges for convenience","Leaving all factory-default ports open in case they are needed later"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Removing default accounts/passwords and disabling unneeded services both directly shrink the number of ways a system can be attacked or misused. Adding more full-privilege administrative accounts for convenience, and leaving unnecessary default ports open "just in case," both work against hardening by expanding, not reducing, the attack surface and the number of accounts that could be compromised.$q$),
('ci-q-1532','cissp',7,'7.3','ordering',2,$q$Order the steps of establishing and maintaining a secure configuration baseline across an environment, from first to last.$q$,$q$["Define the target secure configuration using an accepted hardening standard as a reference","Deploy systems consistently against that baseline, such as via a golden image or automated configuration tool","Periodically scan deployed systems to detect drift from the approved baseline","Remediate any detected drift, whether automatically or through a manual correction process","Formally update the documented baseline itself when a change is legitimately approved through change management"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Configuration management starts with defining what "secure" actually means for a given system type, using recognized hardening guidance; deploying new systems consistently against that definition; continually checking whether live systems still match it; correcting any drift found; and, critically, updating the baseline itself when a change is legitimately approved, so the reference document stays the authoritative target rather than becoming stale relative to intentional, approved evolution of the environment.$q$),
('ci-q-1533','cissp',7,'7.3','matching',2,$q$Match each configuration management term on the left to its correct description on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Baseline configuration","Configuration drift","Golden image","SCAP"],"right":["A standardized specification enabling automated tools to check configuration and vulnerability state against a defined baseline","The documented, approved reference state a system's live configuration is expected to match","A pre-hardened, pre-configured template used to deploy new systems in an identical, known-good state","The gradual, often undocumented divergence of a system's live state away from its approved baseline"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3,2,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$A baseline configuration is the approved target state; configuration drift is the unauthorized divergence from it over time; a golden image is the deployment mechanism that starts every new system already matching that baseline; and SCAP is the standardized protocol that lets automated tools check compliance against the baseline and known vulnerabilities consistently across tools and vendors.$q$);

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 7.1 core (ci-f-1500..1507)
('ci-f-1500','cissp','core',7,'7.1',$$What are the four traditional categories of evidence, and how do they differ?$$,$$Real (tangible physical objects, like a seized drive or weapon), documentary (written or recorded material, like logs or contracts), testimonial (a witness's sworn statement), and demonstrative (something that illustrates or recreates a fact, like a diagram, rather than being the fact itself). Most cases combine several categories to build a complete picture.$$),
('ci-f-1501','cissp','core',7,'7.1',$$What does the best evidence rule require, and when is a duplicate acceptable instead?$$,$$It requires producing the original document (or recording) rather than testimony describing its contents, on the theory that the original is the most reliable proof of what it actually says. A duplicate is accepted when the original was lost, destroyed without bad faith, or is otherwise genuinely unobtainable - not simply because producing it would be inconvenient.$$),
('ci-f-1502','cissp','core',7,'7.1',$$How does the business records exception let routine system logs avoid exclusion as hearsay?$$,$$Hearsay bars out-of-court statements offered to prove what they assert, but records created and kept in the ordinary course of regularly conducted business activity - including system-generated authentication or transaction logs - fall under a recognized exception once a proper foundation (regular practice, contemporaneous creation) is established.$$),
('ci-f-1503','cissp','core',7,'7.1',$$What three criteria does evidence generally need to satisfy to be admissible?$$,$$Relevant (it actually helps prove or disprove a fact at issue), reliable/competent (accurate, properly authenticated, and soundly handled), and legally obtained (no unlawful search, coercion, or rights violation in how it was gathered). Missing any one of the three risks exclusion regardless of how compelling the evidence otherwise appears.$$),
('ci-f-1504','cissp','core',7,'7.1',$$What is Locard's exchange principle, and why does it matter for physical crime scene investigation?$$,$$The forensic-science observation that any contact between two people or objects leaves a mutual trace - a perpetrator carries something away from a scene and leaves something behind. It's the theoretical basis for systematically searching a scene in both directions: for what the intruder took, and for what they left.$$),
('ci-f-1505','cissp','core',7,'7.1',$$What distinguishes lawful enticement from illegal entrapment?$$,$$Enticement offers an opportunity to someone already inclined to break the law - a decoy resource a curious or malicious actor chooses to exploit on their own - and evidence from it is generally usable. Entrapment induces or pressures someone into committing a crime they weren't already predisposed to commit, which can bar prosecution on that basis. The line is drawn at predisposition and inducement, not merely at whether a decoy was involved.$$),
('ci-f-1506','cissp','core',7,'7.1',$$How should live (volatile) forensics and dead-box (static) forensics be combined on a suspect system running active memory-resident malware?$$,$$Capture volatile evidence first - memory contents, running processes, open network connections - since it vanishes the instant power is removed, then power down and create a write-blocked, cryptographically hashed disk image for durable, defensible preservation and analysis. Neither technique alone captures everything a case may need.$$),
('ci-f-1507','cissp','core',7,'7.1',$$What must a search warrant specify to satisfy the particularity requirement, and how does the private-sector search doctrine differ?$$,$$A warrant must describe with reasonable specificity both the place to be searched and the items or evidence sought, preventing open-ended searches beyond what a judge authorized. Separately, constitutional search-and-seizure protections restrict government action - a private employer searching its own equipment under its own policy, with no law-enforcement direction, generally isn't bound by that same constitutional standard, though other laws and contracts may still apply.$$),
-- 7.1 acronym (ci-a-490..492)
('ci-a-490','cissp','acronym',7,'7.1',$$CoC$$,$$Chain of Custody - the unbroken, documented record of who handled a piece of evidence, when, and for what purpose, from collection through presentation in court.$$),
('ci-a-491','cissp','acronym',7,'7.1',$$IOCE$$,$$International Organization on Computer Evidence - an international body that developed widely referenced principles for the reliable handling and exchange of digital evidence across jurisdictions.$$),
('ci-a-492','cissp','acronym',7,'7.1',$$SWGDE$$,$$Scientific Working Group on Digital Evidence - publishes consensus best-practice guidelines for digital evidence collection, handling, and forensic tool validation used across the digital forensics community.$$),
-- 7.1 feynman (ci-y-310..312)
('ci-y-310','cissp','feynman',7,'7.1',$$Explain why a documented chain of custody can get evidence excluded even when nobody actually tampered with it.$$,$$Model answer: A court can't verify what actually happened to a piece of evidence between the moment it was seized and the moment it's presented - all it has is the documented trail claiming to describe that journey. If there's a gap in that trail, say, three days where nobody logged who had custody, the court has no way to distinguish "nothing happened, we just forgot to write it down" from "someone had unsupervised access and could have altered it." The defense doesn't have to prove tampering actually occurred; they only have to show the possibility wasn't ruled out by the documentation. So the exclusion isn't a judgment about the evidence's actual integrity, it's a judgment about whether the paper trail gives the court enough confidence to trust it - which is why meticulous documentation matters as much as the physical handling itself.$$),
('ci-y-311','cissp','feynman',7,'7.1',$$Explain the difference between enticement and entrapment using a fake vulnerable file share as the example.$$,$$Model answer: Imagine a company sets up a fake file share, visible and reachable but doing nothing to actively invite attention, and just watches who connects. If a curious or malicious employee stumbles onto it and decides on their own to poke around and access files they shouldn't, that's enticement - the company offered an opportunity, but the employee's choice to exploit it was entirely their own, driven by an inclination they already had. Now imagine instead that IT staff sent that same employee repeated messages over several weeks, framing it as a fun challenge, insisting nobody would notice, and specifically pushing them to try accessing the restricted files. If that employee had shown no interest in doing anything wrong beforehand, the company didn't just offer an opportunity, it manufactured the crime through persistent pressure. That's entrapment, and using it against someone can bar prosecution, because the law doesn't want the state (or an actor effectively standing in its shoes) generating crime that wouldn't otherwise have happened.$$),
('ci-y-312','cissp','feynman',7,'7.1',$$Explain why forensic investigators image a suspect drive and analyze only the copy, never the original.$$,$$Model answer: Almost any interaction with a live storage device - even just opening a file to look at it - can change metadata like access timestamps, and a sufficiently thorough analysis process risks altering data outright. If investigators worked directly on the original and something changed, however minor, the defense could argue the evidence presented in court isn't the same evidence that was actually seized, undermining the entire case regardless of what the analysis found. By creating a write-blocked, bit-for-bit image and cryptographically hashing both the original and the copy immediately after imaging, investigators can mathematically prove the copy is identical to the original at the moment of seizure, then do all their actual digging on that copy as many times as needed, using as many tools as needed, without ever putting the original's integrity at risk. The original stays sealed away as the untouched reference point that can always be re-verified against the hash if anyone challenges the copy's authenticity later.$$),
-- 7.2 core (ci-f-1508..1515)
('ci-f-1508','cissp','core',7,'7.2',$$What are the core functions a SIEM performs on incoming log data?$$,$$Aggregation (centralizing logs from many sources), normalization (converting varied native formats into a consistent structure), correlation (connecting related events across sources and time), and alerting (flagging matches against rules or behavioral baselines) - turning scattered raw logs into a searchable, actionable security picture.$$),
('ci-f-1509','cissp','core',7,'7.2',$$What are common log source categories a mature SIEM deployment ingests, and what does each typically reveal?$$,$$Firewall/network device logs (permitted or blocked connections), IDS/IPS logs (detected or blocked attack signatures), authentication logs (login attempts and account activity), DNS logs (domain lookups, often revealing malware command-and-control), proxy/web logs (actual destinations and content requested), application and database audit logs (business-layer activity), and endpoint/EDR logs (process and file activity on hosts).$$),
('ci-f-1510','cissp','core',7,'7.2',$$Why does accurate time synchronization (e.g., NTP) across systems matter for log monitoring?$$,$$Reconstructing a trustworthy incident timeline depends on comparing timestamps from multiple systems - a firewall, a server, an IDS - and if their clocks have drifted apart, events can appear to happen in the wrong order relative to each other, undermining correlation and any timeline built from the logs.$$),
('ci-f-1511','cissp','core',7,'7.2',$$What is a clipping level, and why is it used in security monitoring?$$,$$A defined threshold below which routine, expected activity (like an occasional mistyped password) is logged but not alerted on, and above which the same activity triggers an alert because it now looks more like an actual attack than ordinary human error. It keeps analysts from drowning in noise while still catching activity that crosses into genuinely suspicious territory.$$),
('ci-f-1512','cissp','core',7,'7.2',$$How does SOAR extend what a SIEM provides on its own?$$,$$A SIEM detects by aggregating, correlating, and alerting on log data; SOAR consumes those alerts and automatically executes predefined response playbooks - isolating a host, disabling a compromised account, opening a ticket - shrinking the time between detection and containment rather than leaving every response step to a manual analyst.$$),
('ci-f-1513','cissp','core',7,'7.2',$$What does UEBA add to security monitoring that signature- and rule-based detection cannot?$$,$$User and Entity Behavior Analytics builds a statistical baseline of an individual user's or entity's normal behavior - typical login times, locations, data volumes - and flags meaningful deviations from that specific baseline, such as "impossible travel" logins, catching account compromise or insider misuse that wouldn't match any known attack signature.$$),
('ci-f-1514','cissp','core',7,'7.2',$$What are the three data states DLP enforcement is commonly organized around, and where is each typically enforced?$$,$$Data-in-motion (crossing the network, enforced by network DLP inspecting traffic at the perimeter or gateway), data-at-rest (stored in files, databases, or cloud storage, enforced by storage/database or cloud DLP), and data-in-use (actively being accessed on an endpoint - copied, printed, pasted - enforced by endpoint DLP agents watching local operations).$$),
('ci-f-1515','cissp','core',7,'7.2',$$What DLP detection techniques exist for recognizing sensitive content, and when is each best suited?$$,$$Pattern/regex matching detects content that structurally resembles a known format (like an SSN's digit pattern) regardless of source document. Document fingerprinting hashes a specific known file (or sections of it) so DLP recognizes that exact content or a meaningful excerpt elsewhere. Machine-learning classification handles less structured or context-dependent sensitive content that neither simpler technique reliably catches.$$),
-- 7.2 acronym (ci-a-493..495)
('ci-a-493','cissp','acronym',7,'7.2',$$SIEM$$,$$Security Information and Event Management - a platform that aggregates, normalizes, correlates, and alerts on log data centralized from across an environment.$$),
('ci-a-494','cissp','acronym',7,'7.2',$$SOAR$$,$$Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response - automates predefined response playbooks (isolate a host, disable an account) once a SIEM or other tool raises an alert.$$),
('ci-a-495','cissp','acronym',7,'7.2',$$UEBA$$,$$User and Entity Behavior Analytics - flags meaningful deviations from an individual user's or entity's own behavioral baseline, such as impossible-travel logins.$$),
-- 7.2 feynman (ci-y-313..315)
('ci-y-313','cissp','feynman',7,'7.2',$$Explain why SIEM correlation catches attacks that reviewing individual log sources one at a time would miss.$$,$$Model answer: Picture a failed VPN login from a country the user has never connected from, followed minutes later by a successful login and then a large database export. Looked at separately, each event might not even register: one failed login is unremarkable, a successful login after a retry is completely normal, and a large export could just be someone doing their job. Reviewed in isolation across three different log sources - VPN logs, authentication logs, database audit logs - none of them individually screams "attack." But correlated together into a single timeline, the pattern becomes obvious: an account was likely compromised, accessed from an unusual location, and immediately used to exfiltrate data. Correlation's whole value is connecting dots that are individually invisible but collectively unmistakable, which is exactly why a SIEM's job is bigger than just storing logs somewhere searchable.$$),
('ci-y-314','cissp','feynman',7,'7.2',$$Explain why DLP needs different detection techniques for a known confidential document versus a general category like credit card numbers.$$,$$Model answer: A known document, say, an unreleased product design spec, is a specific, fixed piece of content. Document fingerprinting can hash that exact file (or even meaningful chunks of it) so DLP recognizes it, or a copy-pasted excerpt of it, wherever it reappears, even embedded in an unrelated email. But that technique is useless against something like credit card numbers, because there's no single "credit card document" to fingerprint - what makes a credit card number recognizable is its structure, a specific digit pattern and checksum, that could appear in any document, spreadsheet, or chat message the company has never seen before. Pattern/regex matching is built for exactly that: recognizing a category of data by its shape rather than by matching it against a known original. Using fingerprinting for the SSN-shaped data, or regex for the specific design spec, would each miss the thing they weren't designed to catch - which is why real DLP deployments combine multiple techniques rather than picking just one.$$),
('ci-y-315','cissp','feynman',7,'7.2',$$Explain why forwarding logs to a centralized, separate system defeats an attacker who deletes local logs to cover their tracks.$$,$$Model answer: If logs only ever live on the system an attacker has already compromised, then once they have enough access to act, deleting or altering the local log file is trivial - they control the machine, and the evidence of what they did lives on that same machine under their control. Forwarding logs in near-real-time to a separate, centrally managed logging system (often with restricted write/delete access even for that source system's administrators) breaks that assumption: by the time the attacker thinks to cover their tracks, a copy of the relevant events has often already left the compromised host and is sitting somewhere they don't control. They'd have to compromise the central logging infrastructure too, a much larger and usually much better-defended target, to erase the same evidence. Centralization doesn't make logs impossible to tamper with, but it moves the tampering problem to a place the attacker has to work much harder to reach.$$),
-- 7.3 core (ci-f-1516..1522)
('ci-f-1516','cissp','core',7,'7.3',$$What is a baseline configuration, and what purpose does it serve?$$,$$A documented, formally approved reference state defining a system's settings, installed software, patch level, and security controls. It exists so a system's actual live state can be compared against it later to detect unauthorized deviation - the anchor point the whole configuration management discipline is built around.$$),
('ci-f-1517','cissp','core',7,'7.3',$$What is configuration drift, and what typically causes it?$$,$$The gradual, often undocumented divergence of a system's live state from its approved baseline - commonly caused by ad hoc fixes applied outside the change management process, inconsistent patching across a fleet, or one-off troubleshooting changes that never get fed back into the documented baseline.$$),
('ci-f-1518','cissp','core',7,'7.3',$$What do CIS Benchmarks provide, and how are they typically used?$$,$$Consensus-based, vendor-neutral, detailed hardening guidance published by the Center for Internet Security, covering specific secure settings for operating systems, applications, cloud platforms, and network devices - commonly used as the reference standard when defining an organization's own baseline configuration, and checked against with automated compliance scanning.$$),
('ci-f-1519','cissp','core',7,'7.3',$$What is attack surface reduction, and what hardening actions typically implement it?$$,$$Minimizing the number of exposed services, open ports, and enabled accounts an attacker could target - implemented through actions like disabling unused network services, removing default vendor accounts and passwords, and closing unnecessary open ports before a system enters production.$$),
('ci-f-1520','cissp','core',7,'7.3',$$What is a golden (master) image, and what problem does it solve in configuration management?$$,$$A single, fully hardened, pre-configured deployment template - required software installed, unnecessary services disabled, security settings applied - used to build every new system from an identical, already-approved starting state, reducing the inconsistency and early drift that manual, one-off configuration of each new system tends to introduce.$$),
('ci-f-1521','cissp','core',7,'7.3',$$How does declarative configuration management (e.g., Ansible, Puppet, Chef) help combat configuration drift?$$,$$It defines the desired end state in code and continuously (or on a schedule) compares each managed system's live state against that definition, automatically reverting unauthorized changes back to the approved baseline - catching and correcting drift far faster and more consistently than periodic manual configuration audits.$$),
('ci-f-1522','cissp','core',7,'7.3',$$What does the Security Content Automation Protocol (SCAP) standardize?$$,$$A common, machine-readable language and set of specifications for expressing security configuration checks and vulnerability data, so automated tools from different vendors can consistently evaluate whether a system's actual configuration matches a defined baseline standard - standardizing compliance and vulnerability checking across otherwise incompatible tools.$$),
-- 7.3 acronym (ci-a-496..498)
('ci-a-496','cissp','acronym',7,'7.3',$$CIS$$,$$Center for Internet Security - publishes the consensus-based CIS Benchmarks, widely used hardening guides for securely configuring operating systems, applications, and network devices.$$),
('ci-a-497','cissp','acronym',7,'7.3',$$SCAP$$,$$Security Content Automation Protocol - a standardized specification enabling automated tools to check configuration and vulnerability state against a defined security baseline.$$),
('ci-a-498','cissp','acronym',7,'7.3',$$IaC$$,$$Infrastructure as Code - defining and provisioning systems and their configuration through machine-readable definition files rather than manual, one-off setup, enabling consistent, repeatable, and version-controlled deployment.$$),
-- 7.3 feynman (ci-y-316..317)
('ci-y-316','cissp','feynman',7,'7.3',$$Explain why deploying every new server from a golden image reduces configuration drift risk compared to manually configuring each one.$$,$$Model answer: When a human manually configures a new server by following a checklist, every single step is an opportunity for a small mistake or omission - forgetting to disable one service, missing a hardening setting, installing a slightly different software version than last time. Multiply that across dozens or hundreds of servers built over months by different people, and you get a fleet where no two systems are quite identical from day one, even though each admin thought they were following the same baseline. A golden image sidesteps this entirely: the hardening work happens once, gets verified once, and then every new server is a copy of that exact, already-correct state rather than a fresh attempt to recreate it from instructions. It doesn't eliminate drift after deployment, systems can still diverge over time, but it removes the single biggest source of inconsistency, the initial manual build, and gives every system the same accurate starting point to drift from and be measured against.$$),
('ci-y-317','cissp','feynman',7,'7.3',$$Explain why configuration drift is dangerous even when each individual change that caused it seemed harmless at the time.$$,$$Model answer: No administrator sets out to make a system less secure - drift happens because someone needed to fix an urgent problem at 2 AM, opened a port to test something and forgot to close it, or installed a utility to troubleshoot an issue and never removed it afterward. Each of these decisions, in isolation, seemed reasonable and low-risk in the moment. The danger is cumulative and indirect: nobody re-evaluates the system's overall security posture after each small change, so months later the server has quietly accumulated a dozen small deviations that, together, may have opened a real attack path nobody consciously chose to create. Worse, the documented baseline no longer describes what's actually running, so the next security review, incident investigation, or compliance audit is working from inaccurate assumptions about what's actually there. The risk isn't any single change, it's that undocumented small changes compound silently until the live system and the organization's understanding of that system have quietly diverged.$$);
